INT. FLAT 3B - KITCHEN - MORNING

A chirp. One small electronic chirp, somewhere, once.

NADIA, late twenties, in reading glasses, has laid out the check-in inventory across the counter like a battle plan. Highlighter in fist.

KEV, late twenties, eats cereal out of a mug because there are no bowls left that are legally theirs.

NADIA
Ninety minutes. Nine hundred and forty pounds. Go.

KEV
It's not nine forty each?

NADIA
It's nine forty.

KEV
Right, so nine forty each, roughly.

NADIA
Kev.

KEV
Approximately.

Chirp.

INT. FLAT 3B - HALLWAY - MORNING

Nadia holds a lamp up to the wall. In the plaster, at head height, a crater.

KEV
That was the shelf.

NADIA
That was you carrying the shelf.

KEV
The shelf was the instrument.

Nadia produces toothpaste. Squeezes it into the crater and smooths it flat with a library card.

KEV (CONT'D)
That's genius.

NADIA
That's whitening. It'll go grey by lunchtime.

KEV
So we're gone by lunchtime.

NADIA
We're gone by eleven.

INT. FLAT 3B - LIVING ROOM - MORNING

In the corner, a ficus. It is a stick. Every leaf it ever had is in a ring on the carpet around it, like an outline at a crime scene.

KEV
Garden centre opens at nine.

NADIA
The lobby has four.

KEV
The lobby has four of the landlord's.

NADIA
The lobby has four identical ficuses and a broken camera, Kev, I've been past it eleven hundred times.

KEV
That's stealing.

NADIA
It's rehousing.

Chirp.

KEV
What is that?

NADIA
Don't know. Been doing it since Tuesday.

INT. FLAT 3B - HALLWAY - MORNING

The buzzer. Nadia looks at the clock: 9:12.

KEV
He said ten thirty.

NADIA
He said ten thirty.

She presses the intercom.

NADIA (CONT'D)
Brendan! We're just finishing up. Give us five and come straight in.

BRENDAN (V.O.)
I'm in the lift.

NADIA
Then take the stairs, they're lovely.

INT. FLAT 3B - NADIA'S ROOM - MORNING

Nadia digging through a drawer for the spare key. She finds instead her phone, dead, on charge, waking up.

Check-in photos, fourteen months old. She swipes. Stops.

There it is. The crater in the hall wall, in the landlord's own photograph, before they ever moved a box.

She laughs once, out loud.

Then swipes on. The next photo is not the flat. It's a screenshot of a bank transfer. Then another. Then eleven more, monthly, same amount, from her to the letting agency, on the fifth of every month.

Kev's name is not on any of them.

She stands there for a while with the buzzer going.

INT. FLAT 3B - HALLWAY - MORNING

Kev, wrestling the front door open, plaster dust in his hair.

KEV
Right, so my thinking on the ficus is we say it's ours, it's been unwell, we're taking it with us out of loyalty.

NADIA
Sure.

KEV
Sure?

NADIA
Sure, Kev.

He looks at her. Something's off. He hasn't got time to find out what.

BRENDAN, forties, clipboard, enters. He is not unkind. He simply sees everything.

BRENDAN
Morning. Shall we start at the top?

INT. FLAT 3B - HALLWAY - CONTINUOUS

Brendan runs a finger down the inventory. Stops at the wall.

Behind him, slowly, the toothpaste is coming out of the crater in a single white tear.

KEV
Condensation.

BRENDAN
Mint condensation.

KEV
Old building.

Nadia says nothing at all. Kev glances at her, waiting for the save. It doesn't come.

INT. FLAT 3B - LIVING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Brendan crosses to the corner and looks at the ficus, which is now enormous, glossy, and standing in a lobby pot with a brass plaque reading FOSTER & DELL LETTINGS.

Long pause.

BRENDAN
That one does very well by the radiator.

KEV
Doesn't it.

BRENDAN
It's done very well by that radiator for six years.

Chirp.

BRENDAN (CONT'D)
And where's your smoke alarm?

INT. FLAT 3B - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

Nadia opens the third drawer down. Inside, among takeaway sachets, the smoke alarm, face down, chirping.

Kev, behind her, urgent, low.

KEV
Put it in the bin.

NADIA
It's in the inventory.

KEV
Everything's in the inventory, Nadia, that's what an inventory is, put it in the bin.

Nadia takes the alarm out, climbs onto a chair, and screws it back into the ceiling in front of Brendan. It chirps in her hand as she does it.

NADIA
Kev took it down in month one because he burns toast. That's a hundred quid off the deposit and it's fair.

BRENDAN
It's forty.

NADIA
Take it off his half.

KEV
My half.

NADIA
Eleven months, Kev. The fifth of every month.

Kev opens his mouth. Closes it.

BRENDAN
I'll come back for the plant.

INT. FLAT 3B - HALLWAY - LATER

Boxes gone. Brendan gone. The ficus gone. Kev sits on the floor against the mended wall, writing on the back of the check-in inventory.

He hands it up to Nadia. It says I OWE NADIA and a number, and it is signed, and it is even the right number.

NADIA
This isn't legally anything.

KEV
No. It's morally shattering though.

She folds it and puts it in her pocket.

Above them, on the ceiling, the alarm chirps.

NADIA
That's forty quid, that noise.

KEV
That's the cheapest thing in the flat.

They both look up at it and let it chirp.
